# Gemma 4 31B-IT Local Module

Add a new `GemmaLocal.py` module to `modules/LLMs/` that runs [google/gemma-4-31B-it](https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-31B-it) locally using HuggingFace Transformers.

## User Review Required

> [!CAUTION]
> ### Hardware Feasibility — GTX 1660 Ti (6 GB VRAM)
>
> Gemma 4 31B-IT requires **~62 GB in FP16** or **~16-18 GB in 4-bit quantization**. Your GTX 1660 Ti has only **6 GB VRAM**. This means:
>
> | Strategy | VRAM Needed | Feasible? | Speed |
> |---|---|---|---|
> | FP16 (full precision) | ~62 GB | ❌ No | — |
> | 8-bit (`bitsandbytes`) | ~31 GB | ❌ No | — |
> | 4-bit (`bitsandbytes` NF4) | ~16-18 GB | ❌ No | — |
> | 4-bit GPTQ/AWQ quantized variant | ~16-18 GB | ❌ No | — |
> | **CPU offload** (`device_map="auto"` + enough RAM) | 6 GB GPU + **~30 GB+ system RAM** | ⚠️ Maybe | Very slow (~2-5 min/response) |
> | **Full CPU only** (`device_map="cpu"`) | 0 GB GPU, **~35 GB+ RAM** | ⚠️ If RAM allows | Extremely slow |
> | **GGUF via Ollama** (Q4_K_M quant) | ~4-6 GB GPU + RAM | ✅ Best option | Slow but usable |
>
> **Recommended paths (pick one):**
> 1. **Use Ollama with a GGUF quantized Gemma 4 31B** — Your existing `OllamaAI.py` module already supports this. Just run `ollama pull gemma4:31b` and use `OllamaChat(model="gemma4:31b")`. This is the easiest path.
> 2. **Use a smaller Gemma 4 variant** — `google/gemma-4-E4B-it` (4B effective params) fits comfortably in 6 GB and includes audio support.
> 3. **Build the Transformers-based module anyway** — For users with beefier hardware (24+ GB VRAM). The module uses `device_map="auto"` so it will CPU-offload layers automatically if VRAM is insufficient, but will be slow.
>
> **Please confirm which approach you'd like me to implement.** The plan below assumes option **3** (full Transformers module) since it's the most complete and reusable, but I want to flag the hardware reality up front.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> ### `bitsandbytes` on Windows
> The `bitsandbytes` library (used for 4-bit/8-bit quantization) has **limited Windows support**. The latest releases include Windows builds, but stability varies. If quantization fails, the module will fall back to `dtype="auto"` (FP16/BF16) which requires significantly more memory.

## Proposed Changes

### LLMs Module

#### [NEW] [GemmaLocal.py](file:///d:/My%20Business/My%20Projects/On-Going/AI_Toolkit_Hub/modules/LLMs/GemmaLocal.py)

A new class `GemmaLocalChat` following the exact same interface as `GeminiChat`, `OllamaChat`, etc. (`chat()`, `stream()`), plus multimodal capabilities unique to Gemma 4.

**Class design:**

```python
class GemmaLocalChat:
    """Local Gemma 4 provider via HuggingFace Transformers.
    
    Models: google/gemma-4-31B-it, google/gemma-4-E4B-it, google/gemma-4-E2B-it
    """
    DEFAULT_MODEL = "google/gemma-4-31B-it"

    def __init__(self, model, quantize_4bit, enable_thinking, max_image_tokens, device_map)
    def chat(self, prompt, system, temperature, max_tokens) -> str
    def chat_with_image(self, prompt, image_source, system, temperature, max_tokens) -> str
    def stream(self, prompt, system, temperature, max_tokens) -> Generator[str]
    def get_device_info(self) -> dict   # Returns GPU/memory diagnostics
```

**Key implementation details:**

- **Lazy model loading** — Model is loaded on first `chat()`/`stream()` call, not at `__init__`, to avoid blocking imports. Uses a module-level `_model_cache` dict keyed by model ID.
- **HF Token** — Uses existing `HF_TOKEN` from `.env` via `get_api_key("HF_TOKEN")` (Gemma requires accepting the license on HuggingFace).
- **Quantization** — Optional 4-bit NF4 quantization via `BitsAndBytesConfig` if `bitsandbytes` is installed; graceful fallback to `dtype="auto"` if not.
- **Thinking mode** — Controlled via `enable_thinking` param in `apply_chat_template()`. When enabled, uses `processor.parse_response()` to separate thinking from final answer.
- **Sampling** — Defaults follow Gemma 4 best practices: `temperature=1.0`, `top_p=0.95`, `top_k=64`.
- **Multimodal** — `chat_with_image()` accepts a file path or URL, builds the correct message format with `{"type": "image", "url": ...}` before text.
- **Streaming** — Uses `model.generate()` with `TextIteratorStreamer` from `transformers` for true token-by-token streaming.

